{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And an earthen vessel in which it was cooked shall be broken, etc.</b> Rashi explained that the same law applies to all consecrated offerings. Yet the matter still requires explanation: why did the Torah write this law specifically in connection with the sin-offering, which comes to make atonement? And according to the plain sense, we may say that since the Torah testified concerning an earthen vessel that it never loses its absorbed flavor (Pesachim 30a), one might have thought that this is specifically so with lighter consecrated foods that may be eaten for two days and one night, where the food remains in the pot a long time — therefore it absorbs so much that it never loses its absorbed flavor forever; but consecrated offerings of the most sacred order, which may be eaten only for one day and one night, one might think that in so short a time it does not absorb so much and there is a remedy even for an earthen vessel through rinsing — therefore Scripture comes to teach us [that it too must be broken]." "And by way of allusion, which is close at hand to hear, the purification of vessels has a parallel to the purification of the sinner who brings a sin-offering. For just as among vessels there are those that absorb much of the forbidden substance and it cannot be removed through rinsing and they have no remedy except through breaking, and there are those that do not absorb much of the forbidden substance and they have a remedy through rinsing — so too you find a sinner who absorbs much of the forbidden and it is difficult for him to abandon the habit, and he has no remedy except through the breaking of his heart, for it is the vessel in which the forbidden substance was cooked; and you find a person who does not absorb much and has a remedy with the slightest thing, in the manner that our Sages of blessed memory said (Arachin 15b): What is the remedy for one who spreads evil speech? If he is a Torah scholar, let him engage in Torah, as it says (Proverbs 15:4), ״מַרְפֵּא לָשׁוֹן עֵץ חַיִּים״, and if he is an ignoramus, let him humble himself, as it says (ibid.), ״וְסֶלֶף בָּהּ שֶׁבֶר בְּרוּחַ״. And from the sin of evil speech one may learn for every sin and every transgression: when the Temple is not standing, the ignoramus has no remedy except through the breaking of his heart — the vessel in which the sin was cooked; but for the Torah scholar there is a remedy for every sin through the study of Torah. And the sin of the sons of Eli proves that it is not atoned through sacrifice and meal-offering, but it is atoned through the study of Torah (Rosh HaShanah 18a)." "Therefore the Torah wrote the law of vessels in connection with the sin-offering, to tell you that at a time when there is no sacrifice and meal-offering, the purification of the sinner is like the purification of vessels: every ignoramus is likened to an earthen vessel, and it does not lose its absorbed flavor except through the breaking of his heart; and every Torah scholar has the law of a copper vessel, which has a remedy through pouring out and rinsing from inside and from outside — so too every Torah scholar has a remedy through the Torah, which is likened to waters that purify from inside and from outside, for through the Torah his inside becomes like his outside, as it is written, ״מִבַּיִת וּמִחוּץ תְּצַפֶּנּוּ״ (Exodus 25:11), in the manner explained there in parashat Terumah. And this is the matter we have said, that every person resembles some vessel. And there is a proof from what our Sages of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 52b): To what may a Torah scholar before an ignoramus be likened? To a golden vessel. He who converses with him is like a silver vessel; he who derives benefit from him is like an earthen vessel, etc. And it appears that concerning this it was said (Lamentations 4:2), ״בְּנֵי צִיּוֹן הַיְקָרִים הַמְסֻלָּאִים בַּפָּז אֵיכָה נֶחְשְׁבוּ לְנִבְלֵי חֶרֶשׂ מַעֲשֵׂה יְדֵי יוֹצֵר״ — he was lamenting over the men of the treasured people, who were likened to gold and fine gold and hallowed stones, that they multiplied banquets everywhere at the head of every street with the ignorant masses, until they caused exile for themselves and for their children, as they said: whoever multiplies his banquets everywhere, in the end destroys his house, etc.; and it concludes there in the chapter 'These pass over' (ibid. 49a, see there): in the end he goes into exile, etc. And concerning this he said, ״תִּשְׁתַּפֵּכְנָה אַבְנֵי קֹדֶשׁ בְּרֹאשׁ כָּל חוּצוֹת״ — that they multiplied their banquets everywhere with the ignorant masses. And he said immediately: ״בְּנֵי צִיּוֹן הַיְקָרִים״, as it is stated (Proverbs 20:15), ״וּכְלִי יְקָר שִׂפְתֵי דָעַת״; ״הַמְסֻלָּאִים בַּפָּז״ — for at first they were likened to golden vessels, and through the fact that ״תִּשְׁתַּפֵּכְנָה בְּרֹאשׁ כָּל חוּצוֹת״, how were they ״נֶחְשְׁבוּ לְנִבְלֵי חֶרֶשׂ״ — those whole ones who are called the handiwork of the Fashioner of all, for everything was created with one hand, except the whole ones, who were created with two hands (Midrash Tehillim 139:26). And how did they descend from this rung to this lowest depth." "And with this proposal it appears to me possible to settle what our Sages of blessed memory said (Shabbat 105b): Whoever is remiss in the eulogy of a Torah scholar deserves to be buried alive, as it says (Joshua 24:30), ״וַיִּקְבְּרוּ אוֹתוֹ בִּגְבוּל נַחֲלָתוֹ בְּתִמְנַת סֶרַח אֲשֶׁר מִצְּפוֹן לְהַר גָּעַשׁ״ — this teaches that the mountain surged over them to bury them. And whence does he derive to say so — perhaps some other transgression caused the mountain to surge over them? Now, concerning Timnat Serach, Rashi explained it as ״בִּתְמוּנַת חֶרֶשׂ״, meaning the sun, and in Judges (2:9) it is written ״חֶרֶשׂ״ in place of ״סֶרַח״. Now, according to Rashi's approach, it appears to me that ״בְּתִמְנַת״ is from the language of תְּמוּנָה (image/likeness), but ״חֶרֶשׂ״ it appears to me is the language of an earthen vessel, and it means to say that they did not conduct toward him the law of a Torah scholar to eulogize him fittingly, but rather they buried him in the likeness of the rest of the ignorant, who are likened to broken earthenware that never loses its stench and its absorbed flavor — therefore in Judges it reads ״חֶרֶשׂ״ in place of ״סֶרַח״, for the סֶרַח, that is, the stench of his deeds, likens him to an earthen vessel that does not lose its absorbed flavor and its stench except through breaking. But concerning a Torah scholar the measure is not so, for our Sages of blessed memory said (Berakhot 19a): If you saw a Torah scholar committing a transgression at night, do not harbor thoughts about him by day, for he has certainly repented — for presumably he is engaged in Torah, and it is his atonement to purge his sin, and he is purged and rinsed from inside and from outside with the waters of Torah. And even though an ordinary Torah scholar is likened to a golden vessel, from which it is easy to remove a little dust and slight suspicion upon him, nevertheless when he sinned and followed the counsel of the serpent, he was likened to a copper vessel — for the word לָשׁוֹן falls upon לָשׁוֹן, and on account of the fact that his brow was brazen to defy his Creator — and he is purged and rinsed as said above. And from this verse we also learn the laws of purging vessels through use: as is its manner of use, so is its manner of purification."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {3 "The citation from Midrash Tehillim is given as '139:26'; the chapter-verse address may reflect a different numbering system than the standard printed edition."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:36:26.402135464Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}